{
"A replica is a different thing from the original.": "50d9d9ca9732",
"Both sides have a case.": "12861161457c",
"Both sides have a case. Those who say it is the same ship can point out that there was never a day on which Athens had no ship of Theseus. A single plank was changed, and the ship with one new plank was surely the ship that had been there the day before. The next repair was no different. If the ship survives each step, it survives all of them. Those who say it is not the same can point to the result. Not one splinter that Theseus touched is left. A thing made wholly of timber he never saw can be a faithful replica of his ship, and a replica is a different thing from the original.": "53db531fc961",
"Buddhist philosophers met this puzzle early and took a clear position on it. In The Questions of King Milinda, the monk Nāgasena asks the king how he came, and the king says by chariot. Is the pole the chariot? The axle? The wheels? Is it all of them heaped together? Is it something apart from them? To each the king must answer no. Chariot, Nāgasena concludes, is a designation in common use, applied in dependence on the parts, and Nāgasena is a designation of the same kind, applied in dependence on a body, feelings, perceptions, volitions, and consciousness. No separate thing answers to either name.": "46cf4aed547a",
"Few can live with that conclusion.": "0a95d9245ec4",
"Hobbes added a twist that turned an old puzzle into a hard one.": "e372750ca461",
"In 1655 Thomas Hobbes added a twist that turned an old puzzle into a hard one. Suppose that someone had kept the old planks as they were taken out, and afterward put them together again in the same order. Now there are two ships. One stands in the harbor, where the ship of Theseus has always stood, and it grew out of the original by gradual repair. The other stands in a warehouse, and it is made of exactly the planks that Theseus sailed on, arranged as they were. Each has a strong claim to be the original. They cannot both be, since one ship cannot be in two places. And it seems arbitrary to say that neither is.": "55c5cbb8d69b",
"It seems arbitrary to say that neither is the original.": "06679b90371d",
"Just as, when the parts are assembled, there is the word chariot, so, when the aggregates are present, there is the convention a being.": "780b45a4afb8",
"Not one splinter that Theseus touched is left.": "0b9059ae50e8",
"Some care with words is needed first. Two coins from the same mint are the same in one sense, since they are exactly alike, and are plainly two coins. Philosophers call that qualitative identity. The puzzle concerns the other sense, numerical identity, being one and the same thing. The repaired ship and the original are certainly not alike in every respect. The question is whether they are one thing or two. Numerical identity obeys a strict logic. If A is the same thing as B, and B is the same thing as C, then A is the same thing as C. That is why Hobbess version hurts. If the ship in the harbor is the original, and the ship in the warehouse is the original, then the two are one ship, which is absurd.": "f96bef2743e4",
"The first answer says that a thing is its matter. On this view the warehouse ship is the original, and the harbor ship is a new vessel that came into being plank by plank. Strictly applied, the view implies that nothing survives the loss of a single part, so that you are not the person who began reading this sentence. Few can live with that. The second answer says that a thing is its form and its continuous history. Aristotle held that what makes a ship a ship is its organization, and Locke made the same point about living things: their identity depends not on a mass of the same particles, but on something else, so that an oak is the same plant from seedling to great tree as long as it partakes of the same life. On this view the harbor ship wins.": "ebdc0026faff",
"The fourth answer is the most deflating.": "2a1ed968e6bd",
"The puzzle is acted out in real institutions, and different cultures have settled it differently. At Ise in Japan, the principal shrine buildings are torn down and rebuilt on an adjoining plot every twenty years, in new cypress, to the same plan. This has been done more than sixty times since the seventh century. Nobody in Japan thinks that the shrine is twenty years old. Its identity is taken to lie in the form, the site, and the unbroken practice of the carpenters. European conservation long took the opposite view and prized original material above all, so that a restored cathedral was thought to have lost something a ruin kept. In 1994 an international meeting of conservators at Nara, prompted by cases like Ise, agreed that authenticity cannot be judged by one fixed standard and must be understood within each culture. The United States Navy keeps a wooden frigate of 1797, the Constitution, in commission, and only a small fraction of her timber is original. She is still the same ship in the eyes of the law.": "07b8f1fc7a25",
"The puzzle is acted out in real institutions.": "90f8ffbe4e0a",
"The reason the puzzle has lasted is that it is about us. A human body exchanges matter with its surroundings continuously. Most of the atoms in it are replaced within a few years, though some cells, including most of the neurons in the cerebral cortex, last a lifetime. If matter were the standard of identity, the person who was promised something ten years ago would no longer exist to collect it, and the person who committed a crime could not be punished. We hold each other to promises and debts across decades, and so in practice we have all taken the side of form and continuity. Whether we are entitled to is the subject of later lessons on the self.": "4a9071781c58",
"The third answer changes the picture. Suppose a ship is extended in time as well as in space, a long four-dimensional thing of which each days ship is a slice. Then the question of which later ship is the earlier one was badly put. There are two overlapping histories that share their early stages and divide, as two roads may share a stretch of pavement. The fourth answer is the most deflating. It says that there is no further fact to discover. We know everything that happened to every plank. What remains is a decision about how to use the word same, and the decision properly depends on our purposes. A museum, an insurer, and a navy may each be right to count differently.": "6944027034c0",
"They replaced the timber, insomuch that the ship became a standing example.": "8d8458fa4e52",
"This is the fourth answer, conventionalism, and Buddhism accepts the consequence that Western conventionalists often resist: it applies to me. The tradition distinguishes two levels. Conventionally there are ships, chariots, and persons, and it is true to say that this is the shrine founded thirteen centuries ago. Ultimately there are only the parts and their relations, arising and passing in dependence on one another. Abhidharma philosophers went further and held that the parts themselves last only a moment, so that every thing is a ship of Theseus whose planks are replaced continuously. Asked whether the one reborn is the same as the one who died, Nāgasena gives the answer that fits the ship as well: neither the same nor another (na ca so na ca añño). He compares it to a flame that burns through the night. It is one fire in the ordinary sense, though no part of it lasts (Dharma and the West, D2 and D16).": "507e6b4fa0de",
"We hold each other to promises across decades.": "dc1a270264cc",
"With this lesson the subject changes. The last unit asked what we can know. This one asks what there is and what it is like, which is the business of metaphysics, and it begins with a boat. The Athenians believed that they still possessed the ship in which their hero Theseus had sailed back from Crete after killing the Minotaur. Plutarch, writing around 100 CE, reports that it was preserved by the Athenians down even to the time of Demetrius Phalereus, for they took away the old planks as they decayed, putting in new and stronger timber in their place, insomuch that this ship became a standing example among the philosophers, for the logical question as to things that grow; one side holding that the ship remained the same, and the other contending that it was not the same.": "fc7308697529",
"Zhuangzi has an image for the unease this causes. A man hides his boat in a ravine and thinks it safe, yet at midnight a strong man carries it off on his back, and the sleeper knows nothing of it. The strong man is change. One reading of the passage is that nothing stays hidden from it for even a night, and that the boat we find in the morning has already ceased to be the boat we hid.": "c67a84393b24",
